<section class="band" id="architecture">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <div class="kicker">Before you ask</div>
      <h2>The questions your technical evaluator will raise.</h2>
      <p>Answered here so you don't need a call to get to the interesting part.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="faq">
      <div class="q">
        <h4>How is this different from a classifier?</h4>
        <p>A classifier is trained on a fixed label set. Airmino takes <code>options[]</code> as a request parameter, so the same model handles "approve/hold/decline" at 9am and "tier_1/tier_2/tier_3" at 9:05. No retraining, no redeploy.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="q">
        <h4>What's the accuracy versus an LLM?</h4>
        <p>On structured decision tasks we match or exceed prompted GPT-class models on top-1 accuracy, with calibration error under 2%. Full confidence intervals and per-domain breakdowns are in the benchmark appendix.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="q">
        <h4>Can it run inside our infrastructure?</h4>
        <p>Yes. Deploy in your VPC or fully self-hosted. The model is a single container with no external calls — no data leaves your network, no egress costs, no third-party subprocessor.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="q">
        <h4>What's the failure mode?</h4>
        <p>Airmino can only return one of the options you supplied, in a fixed schema. It cannot invent a category or emit free text. When confidence falls below your threshold, it returns <code>escalate: true</code> instead of guessing.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="q">
        <h4>Which workflows does this fit?</h4>
        <p>Anywhere a system must pick one option from a set you define: fraud triage, support ticket routing, loan pre-qualification, offer selection, content-moderation appeal. If the decision fits in a request payload and the answer fits a schema, it fits Airmino.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

